-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"bonus tax rate\\\"}]\\n2f:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"multiple jobs tax\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"multiple jobs tax\\\"}]\\n35:T3d5f,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"\\u003cp\\u003eIf you receive a W-2 from an employer, you might assume estimating your refund is simple since your employer already handles withholding for you. In many ways, that's true. W-2 income is one of the more straightforward situations for tax purposes. But straightforward doesn't mean automatic, and plenty of W-2 employees still end up surprised at tax time because they never checked their numbers ahead of filing.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis guide walks through exactly how W-2 employees can estimate their 2026 federal tax refund, with worked examples and specific dollar amounts. For an instant estimate, use our \\u003ca href=\\\"/tax-refund-calculator\\\"\\u003efree 2026 tax refund calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e designed for W-2 employees.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"table-of-contents\\\"\\u003eTable of Contents\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWhy W-2 Employees Have an Advantage\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eHow to Read Your W-2 Form\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eThe Key Numbers You Need\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e2026 Federal Tax Brackets for W-2 Employees\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eStep-by-Step Refund Calculation\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWorked Example: $60,000 W-2 Employee\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWorked Example: $120,000 Married Filing Jointly\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eCommon Surprises for W-2 Employees\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eOBBBA Changes Affecting W-2 Refunds\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eAdjusting Your W-4 Based on Your Estimate\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eFrequently Asked Questions\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"1-why-w-2-employees-have-an-advantage\\\"\\u003e1. Why W-2 Employees Have an Advantage\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eUnlike freelancers or business owners, W-2 employees have taxes withheld directly from each paycheck throughout the year. Your employer calculates this based on the information you provided on your W-4 form, along with current IRS withholding tables. This built-in system means most W-2 employees have already paid a significant portion, if not all, of what they owe by the time tax season arrives.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis is exactly why W-2 refund calculations tend to be more predictable than self-employment estimates. You're not trying to guess quarterly payments or account for irregular income streams. You're working with a fixed salary, consistent withholding, and a W-2 form that summarizes the entire year in one document.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"2-how-to-read-your-w-2-form\\\"\\u003e2. How to Read Your W-2 Form\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYour W-2 form contains the key numbers you need for an accurate refund estimate. Here's what each relevant box shows:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eBox\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eLabel\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eWhat It Shows\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eBox 1\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eWages, tips, other compensation\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eYour total taxable income for the year\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eBox 2\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eFederal income tax withheld\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eTotal federal tax already taken from your paychecks\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eBox 3\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSocial Security wages\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eIncome subject to Social Security tax (may differ from Box 1)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eBox 4\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSocial Security tax withheld\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e6.2% of Box 3 (up to wage cap)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eBox 5\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eMedicare wages and tips\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eIncome subject to Medicare tax (no cap)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eBox 6\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eMedicare tax withheld\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e1.45% of Box 5\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eBox 12\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eCodes\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eVarious codes (D = 401k, DD = health insurance, etc.)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFor refund estimation, you primarily need Box 1 and Box 2.\\u003c/strong\\u003e Box 1 is your taxable income, and Box 2 is what you've already paid toward your federal tax bill.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"3-the-key-numbers-you-need\\\"\\u003e3. The Key Numbers You Need\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eTo get an accurate refund estimate as a W-2 employee, gather these figures:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eBox 1 wages.\\u003c/strong\\u003e This is your total taxable income for the year, found on your W-2 form. It has already been adjusted for pre-tax contributions like 401(k) deposits or health insurance premiums.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eBox 2 federal withholding.\\u003c/strong\\u003e This shows exactly how much federal tax was already withheld from your paychecks throughout the year. This number is central to your refund calculation.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFiling status.\\u003c/strong\\u003e Whether you file single, married filing jointly, married filing separately, or head of household changes your applicable tax brackets and standard deduction.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDependents and credits.\\u003c/strong\\u003e If you support children or other qualifying dependents, credits like the Child Tax Credit can significantly increase your refund.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eAdditional income.\\u003c/strong\\u003e If you earned freelance income, investment income, or other taxable income outside your W-2 job, you need to include this in your calculation.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"4-2026-federal-tax-brackets-for-w-2-employees\\\"\\u003e4. 2026 Federal Tax Brackets for W-2 Employees\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor 2026, the OBBBA made TCJA brackets permanent. Here are the brackets that apply to your W-2 income:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eRate\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eSingle Filers\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eMarried Filing Jointly\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eHead of Household\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$0 – $11,925\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$0 – $23,850\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$0 – $17,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e12%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$11,925 – $47,750\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$23,850 – $95,500\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$17,000 – $64,850\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e22%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$47,750 – $100,525\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$95,500 – $201,050\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$64,850 – $103,350\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e24%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$100,525 – $191,950\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$201,050 – $383,900\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$103,350 – $197,300\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e32%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$191,950 – $243,725\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$383,900 – $487,450\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$197,300 – $250,500\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e35%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$243,725 – $609,350\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$487,450 – $731,200\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$250,500 – $609,350\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e37%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$609,350+\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$731,200+\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$609,350+\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e2026 Standard Deduction:\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eFiling Status\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eStandard Deduction\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSingle\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$16,100\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eMarried Filing Jointly\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$32,200\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eHead of Household\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$24,150\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cem\\u003eSource: IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-25. For complete details, see our \\u003ca href=\\\"/federal-tax-brackets\\\"\\u003e2026 federal tax brackets guide\\u003c/a\\u003e.\\u003c/em\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"5-step-by-step-refund-calculation\\\"\\u003e5. Step-by-Step Refund Calculation\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHere's the formula every W-2 refund calculator uses:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet your Box 1 wages\\u003c/strong\\u003e from your W-2\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSubtract the standard deduction\\u003c/strong\\u003e for your filing status\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eApply the 2026 tax brackets\\u003c/strong\\u003e to calculate your tax liability\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSubtract any tax credits\\u003c/strong\\u003e (Child Tax Credit, education credits, etc.)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCompare to your Box 2 withholding\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eIf withholding \\u003e tax liability\\u003c/strong\\u003e = you get a refund\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eIf withholding \\u003c tax liability\\u003c/strong\\u003e = you owe money\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"6-worked-example-60-000-w-2-employee\\\"\\u003e6. Worked Example: $60,000 W-2 Employee\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eLet's estimate the refund for a single filer with $60,000 in Box 1 wages and $6,500 in Box 2 federal withholding.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 1: Calculate taxable income\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eBox 1 wages: $60,000\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eStandard deduction (single): −$16,100\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTaxable income: $43,900\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 2: Apply 2026 tax brackets\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e10% on first $11,925 = $1,192.50\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e12% on $11,925–$43,900 = $3,837.00\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTotal federal tax: $5,029.50\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 3: Subtract credits\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eNo dependents, so no Child Tax Credit\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTotal tax liability: $5,029.50\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 4: Compare to withholding\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eTax owed: $5,029.50\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eBox 2 withholding: $6,500\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eEstimated refund: $1,470.50\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis filer would receive approximately \\u003cstrong\\u003e$1,470\\u003c/strong\\u003e as a federal refund.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"7-worked-example-120-000-married-filing-jointly\\\"\\u003e7. Worked Example: $120,000 Married Filing Jointly\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eLet's estimate the refund for a married couple filing jointly with $120,000 combined Box 1 wages, $14,000 federal withholding, and 2 children under 17.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 1: Calculate taxable income\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eBox 1 wages: $120,000\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eStandard deduction (MFJ): −$32,200\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTaxable income: $87,800\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 2: Apply 2026 tax brackets\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e10% on first $23,850 = $2,385\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e12% on $23,850–$87,800 = $7,674\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTotal federal tax: $10,059\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 3: Subtract credits\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eChild Tax Credit (2 children × $2,200): −$4,400\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTotal tax liability: $5,659\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 4: Compare to withholding\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eTax owed: $5,659\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eBox 2 withholding: $14,000\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eEstimated refund: $8,341\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis couple would receive approximately \\u003cstrong\\u003e$8,341\\u003c/strong\\u003e as a federal refund — significantly higher due to the doubled Child Tax Credit under OBBBA.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"8-common-surprises-for-w-2-employees\\\"\\u003e8. Common Surprises for W-2 Employees\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eEven with a relatively predictable system, certain situations catch W-2 employees off guard every year.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eMultiple employers.\\u003c/strong\\u003e If you switched jobs mid-year or worked two jobs simultaneously, each employer withholds taxes independently without knowing about your other income. This can result in under-withholding overall, especially if your combined income pushed you into a higher bracket.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eBonus income.\\u003c/strong\\u003e Bonuses are often withheld at a flat supplemental rate (typically 22%) that doesn't always match your actual tax bracket, leading to either over-withholding or under-withholding depending on your total income level.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eLife changes.\\u003c/strong\\u003e Getting married, having a child, or buying a home can shift your tax situation substantially, but your W-4 withholding might not reflect these changes unless you updated it.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSide income.\\u003c/strong\\u003e Freelance work, rental income, or investment earnings on top of your W-2 job add complexity that your employer's withholding doesn't account for at all.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eOBBBA changes.\\u003c/strong\\u003e The new tip, overtime, and senior deductions may apply to you but aren't automatically reflected in your withholding.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"9-obbba-changes-affecting-w-2-refunds\\\"\\u003e9. OBBBA Changes Affecting W-2 Refunds\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe One Big Beautiful Bill Act introduced several changes that directly affect W-2 employee refunds:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eOBBBA Provision\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eImpact on W-2 Refund\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eTCJA brackets made permanent\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003ePrevents bracket reversion to higher rates\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eChild Tax Credit doubled to $2,200\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eLarger refunds for parents\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSALT cap raised to $40,400\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eLarger refunds for itemizers in high-tax states\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eTip income deduction\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eLarger refunds for tipped workers\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eOvertime pay deduction\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eLarger refunds for hourly workers with overtime\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSenior deduction ($2,000)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eLarger refunds for employees 65+\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"10-adjusting-your-w-4-based-on-your-estimate\\\"\\u003e10. Adjusting Your W-4 Based on Your Estimate\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eOne of the most valuable things a refund estimate does for W-2 employees is highlight whether your current withholding matches your actual tax situation.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eIf your estimate shows a large refund:\\u003c/strong\\u003e You might be over-withholding, essentially giving the government an interest-free loan throughout the year. Adjusting your W-4 to reduce withholding puts that money back in your regular paychecks instead.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eIf your estimate shows you might owe money:\\u003c/strong\\u003e You have time before the filing deadline to update your W-4 for the remainder of the year or increase your withholding to avoid a larger balance due later.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eUse our \\u003ca href=\\\"/irs-withholding-calculator\\\"\\u003eIRS withholding calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e to determine the right withholding amount for your situation.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"11-frequently-asked-questions\\\"\\u003e11. Frequently Asked Questions\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow do I find my federal withholding on my W-2?\\u003c/strong\\u003e Look at Box 2 of your W-2 form. This shows the total federal income tax withheld from your paychecks throughout the year.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhy is my Box 1 income lower than my actual salary?\\u003c/strong\\u003e Box 1 reflects your taxable income after pre-tax deductions like 401(k) contributions, HSA contributions, and pre-tax health insurance premiums. Your actual salary may be higher.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eShould I claim 0 or 1 on my W-4?\\u003c/strong\\u003e The old \\\"allowances\\\" system (0, 1, 2, etc.) was eliminated in 2020. The new W-4 uses a different system. Use our \\u003ca href=\\\"/irs-withholding-calculator\\\"\\u003eIRS withholding calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e to determine the right settings.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow much should I have withheld from each paycheck?\\u003c/strong\\u003e Aim to have enough withheld to cover your expected tax liability, plus a small buffer. The IRS generally requires you to pay 90% of your current year tax or 100% of your prior year tax through withholding.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eIs a big W-2 refund good?\\u003c/strong\\u003e Not necessarily. A large refund means you overpaid throughout the year, giving the government an interest-free loan. Adjusting your W-4 to receive that money in your regular paycheck is usually better.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDoes OBBBA affect my W-2 withholding?\\u003c/strong\\u003e Yes. OBBBA's new deductions (tips, overtime, seniors) may reduce your tax liability, but your employer's withholding tables may not reflect them. You may need to adjust your W-4 to account for these deductions.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003chr/\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"key-takeaways\\\"\\u003eKey Takeaways\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eW-2 employees have a more predictable path to estimating their refund compared to other filing situations, but predictable doesn't mean guaranteed. By reading your W-2 correctly, applying 2026 tax brackets, accounting for OBBBA changes, and using accurate wage and withholding figures, you can get a reliable estimate you can actually plan around.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cem\\u003eThis article is for general informational purposes and does not constitute individualized tax or financial advice. Tax laws change frequently; consult the IRS, your state's department of revenue, or a licensed tax professional for guidance specific to your circumstances.\\u003c/em\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003chr/\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"sources\\\"\\u003eSources\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eIRS — Form W-2 Instructions\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eIRS — Revenue Procedure 2025-25 (2026 inflation adjustments)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eTax Foundation — Analysis of OBBBA provisions (2025)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eIRS — Publication 15-T (Federal Income Tax Withholding Methods)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eIRS — Publication 972 (Child Tax Credit)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"related-calculators\\\"\\u003eRelated Calculators\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/tax-refund-calculator\\\"\\u003eFree 2026 Tax Refund Calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e — Instant refund estimate\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/federal-tax-brackets\\\"\\u003e2026 Federal Tax Brackets\\u003c/a\\u003e — Complete bracket reference\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/irs-withholding-calculator\\\"\\u003eIRS Withholding Calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e — W-4 withholding guidance\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/obbba-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eOBBBA Tax Calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e — Compare 2026 taxes under OBBBA vs prior law\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/paycheck-calculator\\\"\\u003ePaycheck Calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e — Take-home pay for all 50 states\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"related-articles\\\"\\u003eRelated Articles\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/blog/2026-federal-tax-refund-estimator-guide\\\"\\u003e2026 Federal Tax Refund Estimator Guide\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/blog/2026-paycheck-take-home-pay-guide\\\"\\u003e2026 Paycheck \\u0026 Take-Home Pay Guide\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/blog/how-much-tax-will-i-owe-2026\\\"\\u003eHow Much Tax Will I Owe in 2026?\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/blog/2026-federal-tax-brackets-explained\\\"\\u003e2026 Federal Tax Brackets Explained\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/blog/irs-withholding-w4-guide-2026\\\"\\u003eIRS W-4 Withholding Guide\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"30:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-5xl lg:flex lg:gap-8\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"aside\